    || setweight(to_tsvector('english', 'Difficulty: Medium · LeetCode 48
Problem
Rotate an n × n matrix 90° clockwise in-place. You must do this without allocating another matrix.
Understanding the problem
A 90° clockwise rotation maps matrix[r][c] → result[c][n-1-r]. Instead of allocating a new matrix, two sequential in-place operations achieve the same result: first transpose (flip across the main diagonal), then reverse each row.
Intuition
Two in-place operations compose to give a 90° rotation:

① Transpose: swap matrix[r][c] with matrix[c][r] for all r < c.
② Reverse each row: call row.reverse().

No extra memory needed.
Approach
Transpose: for r in range(n), for c in range(r+1, n): swap matrix[r][c] ↔ matrix[c][r].
Reverse rows: for each row in matrix: row.reverse().
Naive Solution — Brute Force
Allocate a second matrix, copy cells to their rotated positions, then copy back.
class Solution:
    def rotate(self, matrix):
        n = len(matrix)

        result = [
            [0] * n
            for _ in range(n)
        ]

        for r in range(n):
            for c in range(n):
                result[c][n - 1 - r] = matrix[r][c]

        for r in range(n):
            for c in range(n):
                matrix[r][c] = result[r][c]
class Solution {
public:
    void rotate(vector<vector<int>>& matrix) {
        int n = matrix.size();

        vector<vector<int>> result(n, vector<int>(n, 0));

        for (int r = 0; r < n; r++) {
            for (int c = 0; c < n; c++) {
                result[c][n - 1 - r] = matrix[r][c];
            }
        }

        for (int r = 0; r < n; r++) {
            for (int c = 0; c < n; c++) {
                matrix[r][c] = result[r][c];
            }
        }
    }
};
class Solution {
    public void rotate(int[][] matrix) {
        int n = matrix.length;

        int[][] result = new int[n][n];

        for (int r = 0; r < n; r++) {
            for (int c = 0; c < n; c++) {
                result[c][n - 1 - r] = matrix[r][c];
            }
        }

        for (int r = 0; r < n; r++) {
            for (int c = 0; c < n; c++) {
                matrix[r][c] = result[r][c];
            }
        }
    }
}
var rotate = function(matrix) {
    const n = matrix.length;

    const result = Array.from({ length: n }, () => new Array(n).fill(0));

    for (let r = 0; r < n; r++) {
        for (let c = 0; c < n; c++) {
            result[c][n - 1 - r] = matrix[r][c];
        }
    }

    for (let r = 0; r < n; r++) {
        for (let c = 0; c < n; c++) {
            matrix[r][c] = result[r][c];
        }
    }
};
