SELECT '55c4ae40-fa10-708d-822c-c5d9d80389b0', '9badfbfe-7312-706c-9422-96438a64c079', 'Command-Line Reference', 'Command-Line Reference', 'Every networking command worth knowing from a terminal, with what it actually shows you.
Find your public IP address
$ curl ifconfig.me -s
# returns your machine''s public-facing IP address
Ping — round-trip time & packet loss
$ ping google.com

PING google.com (142.250.77.238): 56 data bytes
64 bytes from 142.250.77.238: icmp_seq=0 ttl=118 time=8.518 ms
64 bytes from 142.250.77.238: icmp_seq=1 ttl=118 time=9.610 ms
...
--- google.com ping statistics ---
11 packets transmitted, 11 packets received, 0.0% packet loss
round-trip min/avg/max/stddev = 7.685/9.245/11.478/1.016 ms
What it measures
Ping measures the round-trip time (RTT) for a small message sent from your machine to a destination and echoed straight back — it''s the fastest way to check if a host is reachable and how much latency exists on the path. The ttl=118 shown is the Time-To-Live value remaining when the reply arrived, and 0.0% packet loss confirms every packet round-tripped successfully.
DNS lookup
$ dig google.com          # full DNS resolution trace
$ man dig                 # manual / all available flags
$ dig -type=mx gmail.com  # find a domain''s mail servers (MX records)
Packet capture
$ tcpdump -c 5
# captures the next 5 packets crossing a network interface, live
Quick reference table
Command	Purpose
curl ifconfig.me -s	Show public IP address
ping <host>	Test reachability + measure RTT
dig <domain>	DNS lookup / resolution trace
tcpdump -c N	Capture N live packets',
  setweight(to_tsvector('english', 'Command-Line Reference'), 'A')
    || setweight(to_tsvector('english', 'Command-Line Reference'), 'B')
    || setweight(to_tsvector('english', 'Every networking command worth knowing from a terminal, with what it actually shows you.
Find your public IP address
$ curl ifconfig.me -s
# returns your machine''s public-facing IP address
