At the simplest level, when Computer A wants to talk to Computer B — possibly on the other side of the planet — the message doesn''t teleport. It is broken down, addressed, and physically carried across a chain of intermediate devices until it reaches the destination, and a reply makes the same kind of journey back.
Scenario — typing google.com
You type google.com in a browser and hit enter → your computer turns that into a request → the request is sent through your router, your ISP, and a chain of other networks → it reaches Google''s server → the server sends back a response (the webpage) → your browser renders it. This client → server round trip is the basic shape of almost everything that happens on a network.'), 'C'),
